{"name":"Chain Mail","hex":"#81777f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chain mail","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flexible defensive armor, made of a mesh of interlinked metal rings."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chain letter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chain_mail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chain letter","blocks":[{"text":"A chain letter is a message that ostensibly attempts to convince the recipient to make a number of copies and pass them on to a certain number of recipients. The \"chain\" is an exponentially growing pyramid that cannot be sustained."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_letter","wikidata":"Q371008"},{"title":"Chain mail","blocks":[{"text":"Mail is a type of armour consisting of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh. It was widely used in European militaries from the 3rd century BC to the 16th century AD, while in Asia, Africa, and the Middle East it remained in military use until 18th century. Chain mail is still in use in industries such as butchery and as protection against the powerful bites of creatures such as sharks. A coat of this armour is often called a hauberk or sometimes a byrnie."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Cotte_de_Maille%2C_El_Bayadh%2C_Alg%C3%A9rie%2C_17%C3%A8me_si%C3%A8cle.jpg/330px-Cotte_de_Maille%2C_El_Bayadh%2C_Alg%C3%A9rie%2C_17%C3%A8me_si%C3%A8cle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_mail","wikidata":"Q659631"},{"title":"Chain","blocks":[{"text":"A chain is a serial assembly of connected links typically made of metal, with an overall character similar to that of a rope in that it is flexible and curved in compression, but linear, rigid, and load-bearing in tension. A chain may consist of two or more links. Chains can be classified by their design, which can be dictated by their use:"},{"items":["Those designed for lifting, such as when used with a hoist; for pulling; or for securing, such as with a bicycle lock, have links that are torus-shaped, which make the chain flexible in two dimensions. Small chains serving as jewellery are a mostly decorative analogue of such types.","Those designed for transferring power in machines have links designed to mesh with the teeth of the sprockets of the machine, and are flexible in only one dimension. They are known as roller chains, though there are also non-roller chains such as block chains."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Broad_chain_closeup.jpg/330px-Broad_chain_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain","wikidata":"Q2397485"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Chain_Letter.jpg/500px-Chain_Letter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Chain_Letter.jpg/500px-Chain_Letter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Printed Australian chain letter from 2006, with a five-cent coin taped to it as what it calls a \"financial eye-catcher\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChain_Letter.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Himmelsbrief_christian_text_colored_angel_1800.jpg/500px-Himmelsbrief_christian_text_colored_angel_1800.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Himmelsbrief_christian_text_colored_angel_1800.jpg/500px-Himmelsbrief_christian_text_colored_angel_1800.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An example of a \"Heaven Letter\" (Himmelsbrief) from around 1800","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHimmelsbrief_christian_text_colored_angel_1800.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Fukou_no_tegami_20240913.jpg/500px-Fukou_no_tegami_20240913.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Fukou_no_tegami_20240913.jpg/500px-Fukou_no_tegami_20240913.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A modern example of a handwritten \"Fukou no Tegami\" (Unlucky Letter)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFukou_no_tegami_20240913.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-11-20_2.jpg/500px-Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-11-20_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-11-20_2.jpg/500px-Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-11-20_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An English-language \"Lucky Letter\" reported in the Tokyo Asahi Shimbun, 20 November 1922","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-11-20_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-01-27_01.jpg/500px-Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-01-27_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-01-27_01.jpg/500px-Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-01-27_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Japanese \"Lucky Letter\" titled \"For Good Fortune\" (幸運之為に), featured in the Tokyo Asahi Shimbun, 27 January 1922","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-01-27_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-02-26.jpg/330px-Tokyo_Asahi_Shinbun_1922-02-26.jpg?utm_